<Base title="Pet registries by country" description="Which pet registry applies in Italy, Germany, Austria, Switzerland and France, who registers, the deadlines, the fees and who to call if your animal is lost.">
  <article class="wrap narrow hero prose">
    <h1>Pet registries by country</h1>
    <p class="lead">A microchip on its own does not say who you are. The registry does. Here is which registry applies in Italy, Germany, Austria, Switzerland and France, who files the entry, the deadlines you have to meet and who to call when your animal goes missing. Rules change: where we could not verify a number, we say so.</p>

    <h2>Italy</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Registry</th><td>Anagrafe degli animali d'affezione, now the national SINAC (Sistema informativo nazionale degli animali da compagnia), run by the Ministry of Health with the regions (D.Lgs. 134/2022, DM 2 November 2023). Several regions still publish their own rules on top of it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Mandatory for</th><td>Dogs everywhere. Cats depend on the region: mandatory in Lombardia since 2020; in Friuli Venezia Giulia from 1 July 2026 (regional notice); in Veneto and Lazio only voluntary, unless the cat comes from outside the region or is sold commercially. Ferrets: voluntary in most regions.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Who registers</th><td>An ASL/ATS public veterinarian or an accredited private veterinarian, who implants the chip and enters the animal. You can file loss, found and death reports yourself in the SINAC citizen portal with SPID or CIE (Piemonte, for example).</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline after birth / acquisition / change of owner / moving</th><td>Varies by region. Friuli Venezia Giulia: by day 60 of life, 10 days after acquisition, in any case before the dog is handed over; owner change 10 days; new address 30 days. Lombardia: 30 days from birth or 15 days from possession; owner change or move 15 days. Lazio: 2 months from birth or 10 days from possession; move 15 days.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline to report loss / death</th><td>Also regional. Lombardia: loss or theft 7 days, death 15 days. Friuli Venezia Giulia: loss 5 days, death 30 days. Lazio: loss 15 days, death 5 days. Check your own region.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>What you need</th><td>Microchip, your ID and tax code (codice fiscale), proof of address. For a loss: the police or Carabinieri report where the region asks for one.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Fees</th><td>Set regionally. Examples: Lazio €28 for chip and entry at the ASL, €8 for entry by an accredited vet; Friuli Venezia Giulia €5 certificate fee at a public vet plus the chip. Private vets charge their own price.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Official link</th><td><a href="https://sinac.vetinfo.it/" rel="noopener">sinac.vetinfo.it</a> and your regional veterinary service</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Germany</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Registry</th><td>No federal registry and no federal duty to register. Two private, free registries cover most animals: TASSO e.V. and FINDEFIX (Deutscher Tierschutzbund). Some Länder run their own compulsory dog registers, for example Berlin (Hunderegister, HundeG) and Niedersachsen (Zentrales Register, NHundG).</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Mandatory for</th><td>Nationally: nothing. Berlin: all dogs, chip from 3 months. Niedersachsen: all dogs. Other Länder: often only listed or "dangerous" dogs. Cats: never mandatory, but register them anyway.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Who registers</th><td>You, online. The vet implants the chip but usually does not register it for you.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline after acquisition / change of owner / moving</th><td>TASSO and FINDEFIX: no deadline, do it the day of chipping. Berlin and Niedersachsen: the law sets a deadline for new dogs and new residents; the registry sites we loaded do not state it, so check with your Ordnungsamt.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline to report loss / death</th><td>No legal deadline for the private registries; report a loss immediately, a death when it happens so the entry closes. Land registers: deregister yourself online or by phone (free in Berlin).</td></tr>
        <tr><th>What you need</th><td>Chip number (15 digits), your address and phone. Land registers also ask for your ID data and, in Berlin, the entry doubles as dog-tax registration since 2024.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Fees</th><td>TASSO and FINDEFIX: free. Berlin: €17.50 online, €26.50 by phone or form. Niedersachsen: €14.50 online, €23.50 by form or phone.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Official link</th><td><a href="https://www.tasso.net/" rel="noopener">tasso.net</a> · <a href="https://www.findefix.com/" rel="noopener">findefix.com</a> · <a href="https://hunderegister.berlin.de/" rel="noopener">hunderegister.berlin.de</a> · <a href="https://www.hunderegister-nds.de/" rel="noopener">hunderegister-nds.de</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Austria</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Registry</th><td>Heimtierdatenbank, the federal database run by the Ministry of Social Affairs and Health (Tierschutzgesetz §24a). Entries in a private database or the municipal dog-tax list do not count.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Mandatory for</th><td>All dogs. Cats only if used for breeding. Pet cats: voluntary.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Who registers</th><td>You online with ID Austria, the vet at chipping (since 2025 the vet must enter the dog and your ID data, for a fee), the Bezirksverwaltungsbehörde (veterinary department, fee) or some municipalities.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline after birth / acquisition / moving / change of owner</th><td>Puppies chipped by 3 months and before any handover. Register within 1 month of chipping, entry into Austria or taking the dog over, and always before passing it on. Every change (address, new keeper) must be reported "immediately"; the law gives no day count.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline to report loss / death</th><td>Death must be entered; no day count in §24a. Loss: no legal deadline, report at once to the database and the police.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>What you need</th><td>Chip number, official photo ID (type and number are recorded), address, date of birth, date you took the dog. From 1 July 2026 also the Sachkundenachweis if the Land requires one.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Fees</th><td>Online with ID Austria: free. Chip, vet entry and authority entry: fees set by the vet or the district; no official amount is published, ask first. Fines for non-registration up to €3,750.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Official link</th><td><a href="https://www.oesterreich.gv.at/themen/reisen_und_freizeit/haustiere/1/Seite.741000.html" rel="noopener">oesterreich.gv.at, Registrierung von Hunden</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Switzerland</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Registry</th><td>AMICUS, the national dog database run by Identitas AG for the cantons (Tierseuchenverordnung art. 16–18). ANIS, also Identitas, is the voluntary registry for cats and other pets.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Mandatory for</th><td>Dogs. Cats and others: voluntary (ANIS).</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Who registers</th><td>Your municipality registers you as a dog owner first. The vet implants the chip and enters the dog. Seller records the handover in AMICUS, buyer confirms it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline after birth / acquisition / moving / change of owner</th><td>Puppies chipped within the first 3 months and before any handover. Imported dog: chip checked by a vet within 10 days of entry. Owner change: complete once the buyer confirms; no day count given. Moving: tell your municipality; moving abroad is recorded as an export.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline to report loss / death</th><td>No deadline in AMICUS. Enter the death yourself (or the vet or municipality does). Loss: report to STMZ, the Swiss lost-and-found centre, which forwards it to the cantonal office.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>What you need</th><td>Chip number, owner registration from your municipality, current phone and email in AMICUS.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Fees</th><td>Not published on the AMICUS site; vet and municipal fees vary. ANIS: check with your vet or the registry.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Official link</th><td><a href="https://www.amicus.ch/" rel="noopener">amicus.ch</a> · <a href="https://www.anis.ch/" rel="noopener">anis.ch</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>France</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Registry</th><td>Fichier national d'identification des carnivores domestiques, managed by I-CAD under delegation from the Ministry of Agriculture.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Mandatory for</th><td>Dogs older than 4 months, cats older than 7 months, and ferrets. Any dog, cat or ferret must be identified before it is sold or given away, whatever its age.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Who registers</th><td>A veterinarian (or authorised tattooer) identifies the animal and files it with I-CAD within 8 days. You handle later changes in your I-CAD owner account.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline after acquisition / change of owner / moving</th><td>The person who hands the animal over must declare the change of owner to I-CAD within 8 days. New address or phone: you must update I-CAD; no day count is published, do it when you move.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deadline to report loss / death</th><td>No published deadline. Declare the loss in your I-CAD account at once: an animal picked up by the fourrière is held only 8 working days before it can be rehomed. Declare a death through the same account.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>What you need</th><td>Chip or tattoo number, your identification card for the animal, ID and address.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Fees</th><td>Identification is paid to the vet. I-CAD's own fees for changes were not published on the pages we loaded (they change on 1 July 2026): check with I-CAD.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Official link</th><td><a href="https://www.i-cad.fr/" rel="noopener">i-cad.fr</a> · <a href="https://www.service-public.gouv.fr/particuliers/vosdroits/F34877" rel="noopener">service-public.gouv.fr</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>When you move between these countries</h2>
    <ul class="paw-list">
      <li><strong>To Italy:</strong> register the dog in your new region's registry; deadlines are regional (Friuli Venezia Giulia 10 days from arrival, Veneto 10 days, Lazio 10 days from possession). Cats too in Lombardia.</li>
      <li><strong>To Germany:</strong> register with TASSO or FINDEFIX right away; in Berlin and Niedersachsen also with the Land register (deadline: ask the Ordnungsamt).</li>
      <li><strong>To Austria:</strong> enter the dog in the Heimtierdatenbank within 1 month of arrival.</li>
      <li><strong>To Switzerland:</strong> register as owner with your municipality, have a vet read the chip and enter the dog within 10 days of entry.</li>
      <li><strong>To France:</strong> have a French vet record the animal in I-CAD; the vet files within 8 days. Foreign chips are accepted, the file is not.</li>
      <li><strong>Leaving:</strong> close the entry in the old country (Switzerland records an export; Italy, Austria and France treat it as a change you must report; Germany's private registries keep working abroad).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If your animal is lost: who to call</h2>
    <ul class="paw-list">
      <li><strong>Italy:</strong> report in the SINAC citizen portal (SPID/CIE) or to your ASL/ATS veterinary service, and to the local police; your region may require the police report within a few days.</li>
      <li><strong>Germany:</strong> TASSO hotline +49 6190 937300 (24 h) or FINDEFIX +49 228 6049635 (24 h). Also notify the local shelter and Ordnungsamt.</li>
      <li><strong>Austria:</strong> enter the loss in the Heimtierdatenbank (ID Austria) and tell the police and the nearest shelter. Anyone can check a chip number on the database's public search.</li>
      <li><strong>Switzerland:</strong> STMZ, lost line 0900 357 358 (charged), found line 0848 357 358 (24 h, free online). Keep your contacts in AMICUS or ANIS current.</li>
      <li><strong>France:</strong> declare the loss in your I-CAD account and post a Filalapat notice; I-CAD phone 09 77 40 30 77 (Mon–Fri). Call the local fourrière the same day.</li>
      <li><strong>Across borders:</strong> <a href="https://www.europetnet.org/" rel="noopener">Europetnet</a> searches member registries in 26 countries by chip number. I-CAD, TASSO, AMICUS and ANIS are members; the Italian and Austrian federal registries are not, so for those use the national search.</li>
    </ul>

